---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { href, site } from '../data/site';
---
<Layout title="Developers" section="Developers" description="The engineering approach, build workflow, efficiency targets and repository access model behind ContinuumOS.">
  <section class="page-intro wrap"><span class="section-label">FOR DEVELOPERS / MODERN SYSTEMS, MEASURED CLAIMS</span><h1>Build with intent.<br /><span class="mint">Verify the boundary.</span></h1><p class="lead">Original systems engineering in Rust, explicit protocols between replaceable components and a development loop designed around retaining work.</p></section>
  <div class="page-content wrap"><section class="two-column article-block"><div><span class="section-label">THE FOUNDATION</span><h2>Small steps.<br />Real artifacts.</h2><p>The current implementation uses a freestanding original Rust kernel and narrowly documented architecture-specific assembly. Windows-native orchestration builds and packages a standalone x86-64 UEFI ISO.</p><p>P1-02 uses pinned local QEMU, OVMF and ISO tools. Bounded, offline emulator runs retain serial and artifact evidence. Existing compilers, firmware and boot tools are dependencies; their use does not replace the kernel.</p></div><div class="target-panel"><span class="micro">CURRENT PUBLICATION MODEL</span><h3 style="margin-top:20px">Public explanations.<br />Restricted OS source.</h3><p>The OS repository and full engineering library are private. This public repository contains only the website and its browser concepts. No public OS checkout or binary release is available here.</p><a class="text-link" href={site.repository} style="margin-top:25px">Website repository <span>↗</span></a></div></section>
    <section class="article-block" style="margin-top:75px"><span class="section-label">THE WORKFLOW</span><h2>Host tools first. Guest claims separately.</h2><p>OS builds require repository access, the project’s pinned workspace-local Rust toolchain, existing MSVC/Windows SDK prerequisites and the supplied QEMU/OVMF/ISO inputs. A host compile does not prove that a dependency runs inside the guest.</p><div class="two-column" style="margin-top:25px"><div><span class="code-caption">OS MAINTAINERS WITH PRIVATE REPOSITORY ACCESS</span><pre class="code-block"><code>{`. ./tools/Enter-Dev.ps1
cargo xtask doctor --profile m1
cargo xtask build --profile dev
cargo xtask iso --profile m1
cargo xtask test --suite m1 --case iso-smoke`}</code></pre><p style="font-size:12px">Run from the private OS checkout using its documented prerequisites. These commands do not operate on this website repository.</p></div><div><span class="code-caption">THIS PUBLIC WEBSITE · NODE 24.14+ · NPM</span><pre class="code-block"><code>{`git clone https://github.com/Continuum-Foundry/continuumos-site.git
cd continuumos-site
npm ci
npm run dev

# Before publishing
npm run check
npm run build`}</code></pre><p style="font-size:12px">The site is static HTML, CSS and browser TypeScript. It requires no guest runtime or backend service.</p></div></div></section>
    <section id="efficiency" class="article-block two-column" style="margin-top:75px"><div><span class="section-label">EFFICIENCY / A DESIGN CONSTRAINT</span><h2>Idle should mean idle.</h2><p>The proposed desktop avoids perpetual rendering when nothing changes. Software composition comes first; visual effects must justify their cost and retain a reduced-effects path.</p><p>Cold-idle accounting includes the kernel, services, app shells, framebuffers and bounded caches. Configuring a 2 GiB VM is capacity, not permission to consume it at idle. Guest performance is not yet measured.</p></div><div class="target-panel"><span class="micro">PROVISIONAL TARGET · NOT A MEASUREMENT</span><div class="target-value">256 <span>MiB</span></div><h3 style="margin-top:10px">Cold-idle guest memory</h3><p>Initial reference direction: one vCPU, software composition and a declared display profile. Optional inference requires separate cold, warm and active accounting.</p></div></section>
    <section class="article-block"><span class="section-label">INTERFACES BEFORE INTEGRATIONS</span><h2>Portability is a question to test.</h2><p>No guest compiler, GPU stack, third-party crate port or Linux application compatibility is assumed. Versioned wire protocols cross replaceable boundaries; Rust’s native memory layout is not a service contract.</p><p>The UI toolkit comparison remains open. Typed actions are required platform infrastructure. Optional inference follows the first state-preserving guest reload and never gates the AI-free core.</p><a class="text-link" href={href('architecture/')} style="margin-top:25px">Read the architecture <span>↗</span></a></section>
    <div class="note"><h3>Rights and contributions</h3><p>Original website and OS material is all rights reserved. Public visibility is not an open-source license. External patches are currently paused; third-party packages retain their own licenses. See this website repository’s LICENSE and notices.</p></div>
  </div>
</Layout>
